-- title:
--   Pole charts of j and j(q^q) compare, q=3
-- statement:
--   Let $q$ be a prime with $q=3$, let $M'\ge 1$ with $q\nmid M'$, and assume $\overline{\mathbb Q}\cdot F(\mathrm{X}_{\mathrm{full}}(M'))\le F:=\overline{\mathbb Q}\cdot F(X_H(q^2M'))$, where $H\le(\mathbb Z/q^2M')^\times$ is the kernel of reduction to $(\mathbb Z/q)^\times$ and both fields are realised as intermediate fields of $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ in $\mathrm{LaurentSeries}(\overline{\mathbb Q})$ via coefficientwise extension `coeffEmb`. Let $k_0\subseteq\overline{\mathbb Q}$, let $K_1$ be a finite extension of $k_0$ inside $\overline{\mathbb Q}$, and let $A_1$ be a valuation subring of $K_1$. Give $F$ its $k_0$-algebra structure through the constants, and let $F_0$ be a $k_0$-intermediate field of $F$ such that: the compositum of $F_0$ with the $k_0$-subfield generated by all constants is $F$; $F_0$ is stable under every $\mathrm{levelAutBar}\,q\,M'\,\zeta'\,\gamma$ with $\zeta'$ a primitive $q$-th root of unity in $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ and $\gamma\in\Gamma_0(M')$; for every finite $k_0$-subextension $K'$ of $\overline{\mathbb Q}$, any family $c:\mathrm{Fin}\,m\to\overline{\mathbb Q}$ that is $K'$-linearly independent is linearly independent over the compositum $T(K')$ of $K'$ and $F_0$ inside $F$, in the sense that $\sum_i c_i a_i=0$ with all $a_i\in T(K')$ forces every $a_i=0$; and every element of $F$ whose Laurent series has rational coefficients lies in $F_0$. Write $T_1:=T(K_1)$ and assume $T_1$ carries an $A_1$-algebra structure whose structure map is the inclusion $A_1\subseteq K_1\subseteq\overline{\mathbb Q}\to F$. Let $j_1\in T_1$ be nonzero with Laurent series the image of $j$ (the series `jq`), and for $j\in T_1$ write $C(j)$ for the integral closure of $A_1[j^{-1}]$ in $T_1$, that is, `chartAlgInf`. Then there is $j'\in T_1$ whose Laurent series is the image of $\mathrm{qExpand}\,q$ applied to `jq` (the series obtained from $j$ by multiplying all exponents by $q$) such that every $y\in C(j')$ admits $s=1+j_1^{-1}a$ with $a\in C(j_1)$, $s\in C(j_1)$ and $sy\in C(j_1)$, and symmetrically every $y\in C(j_1)$ admits $s=1+j'^{-1}a$ with $a\in C(j')$, $s\in C(j')$ and $sy\in C(j')$.
--
--   This transports the classical integrality and symmetry properties of the modular equation relating $j(\tau)$ and $j(q\tau)$ to the two-chart integral model over the level field $T_1$, showing that the pole-chart rings attached to $j_1$ and to $j'$ dominate each other after multiplication by units congruent to $1$ modulo the uniformiser $j^{-1}$. It is the $q=3$ instance used on the Igusa-tower leg of the semistable covering construction, and is invoked in the analysis of centred points and of level automorphisms acting on the two-chart integral model.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_ModularCurve_FullLevel_exists_qExpand_forall_mem_chartAlgInf_exists_mul_mem_levelField_of_eq_three.lean

